\title[External hints are priced linearly in bits]{External Hints Are Priced Linearly in Bits:\\ A Master Law, a Which-Factor Ceiling,\\ and a Strategy-Free Guessing Bound}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 9, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study how much a \emph{filter}, meaning a prediction of where a hidden object lies, can accelerate an exhaustive search. A hit costs a fraction $\theta$ of the full work and a miss costs the full work. On an arbitrary finite probability space the speedup obeys the \emph{master law} $\Sp = 1/(1-(1-\theta)\Phit)$, so every filter acts only through its hit probability. On product spaces whose hidden label is uniform on the fibres of the observable coordinate, we locate the \emph{symmetry break} between internal and external information. Every reading generated from the observable coordinate has $\Phit = 1/K$, which at $K=2$, $\theta=1/2$ is exactly the known $4/3$ cap. An external hint whose likelihood lives on the label coordinate survives verbatim. For a hint about one of two factors that does not name its factor, we prove the sharp \emph{which-factor ceiling} $2K^2/(K^2+1)<2$ for every dial size and every likelihood, with binary-dial form $8/(7-2\alpha)$. For $r$ factors the fine-dial limit is $r/(r-1)$, the ceiling overshoots for $r\ge3$, and for $r\ge7$ the binary dial is globally optimal with value $4r/(3r-1)\to4/3$. We analyse a certain-hint ladder (exactly two bits lost), trace hints (bounded recovery divisors keep rate one), and a noise break-even surface (external hints tolerate strictly more noise). Finally we prove a \emph{strategy-free guessing bound}: for a uniform target among $M$ candidates and any hint with $|B|$ values, every strategy needs at least $\tfrac12(M/|B|+1)$ guesses on average. Hence a $t$-bit hint buys at most $2^t$. The bound is sharp, there is no synergy, and isolation costs $\lceil\log_2M\rceil$ queries.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

A hidden object (a key, a password, a prime factor) lies among many candidates, and an exhaustive search costs one unit of work. A \emph{filter} predicts which part of the space holds the object. If it is right we pay $\theta<1$; if it is wrong we pay the full unit. The motivating instance is factoring $N=pq$. Candidate primes are sorted into $K$ classes (a \emph{dial}), and knowing the class of $p$ cuts the work to $\theta=1/K$. \emph{Internal readings} are computed from public data, ultimately from $N$. \emph{External hints} come from outside: side channels, leaked bits, oracles.

Earlier work established that residue-based internal filters are capped at $4/3$, and companion experiments measured positional information at about $5.19\times$. This paper completes the picture with the third row, external information.

\section{The master law}

\begin{definition}[Filtered search]
Let $(\Omega,\mu)$ be a finite probability space, $\mathrm{hit}\subseteq\Omega$, and $\theta\in\mathbb R$. Put $\Phit=\sum_{\omega\in\mathrm{hit}}\mu(\omega)$, $\mathrm{Work}=\sum_\omega\mu(\omega)c(\omega)$ with $c=\theta$ on hits and $c=1$ on misses, and $\Sp=1/\mathrm{Work}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Master law]\label{thm:master}
$\mathrm{Work}=1-(1-\theta)\Phit$ and $\Sp=S_\theta(\Phit)$, where $S_\theta(P):=1/(1-(1-\theta)P)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pointwise $\mu c=\mu-(1-\theta)\mu\mathbf 1_{\mathrm{hit}}$. Sum and use $\sum\mu=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[One scalar prices everything]
Two filters, possibly on different spaces, with equal hit probabilities have equal speedups.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:shape}
Let $0<\theta<1$. Then $\Phit\in[0,1]$; $S_\theta$ is strictly increasing on $[0,1]$; $S_\theta(P)\le1/\theta$ for $P\le1$; and $S_\theta(P)=1/\theta$ iff $P=1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The denominator is positive and strictly decreasing in $P$. Moreover $1-(1-\theta)P-\theta=(1-\theta)(1-P)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The symmetry break}

\begin{definition}[Fibre-uniform product model]
Let $C$ be finite with law $\nu$ and $K\ge1$. A kernel $R(c,b,h)\ge0$ with $\sum_hR(c,b,h)=1$ defines the law $\mathbb P(c,b,h)=\nu(c)R(c,b,h)/K$ on $C\times[K]\times[K]$, so the label $b$ is uniform on each fibre of $c$. A hit is $h=b$.
\end{definition}

For every kernel,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:phit}
\Phit=\sum_c\frac{\nu(c)}{K}\sum_bR(c,b,b).
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Internal readings die on fibre uniformity]\label{thm:internal}
If $R(c,b,h)=R_c(h)$ with $\sum_hR_c(h)=1$ (an arbitrary $c$-dependent randomisation), then $\Phit=1/K$. For $K=2$, $\theta=1/2$ the speedup is exactly $4/3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
In \eqref{eq:phit} the inner sum is $\sum_bR_c(b)=1$. Then $S_{1/2}(1/2)=1/(1-1/4)=4/3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[External likelihoods survive verbatim]\label{thm:external}
If $R(c,b,h)=L(b,h)$, then $\Phit=\frac1K\sum_bL(b,b)$, independent of $\nu$. A perfect hint has $\Phit=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The inner sum in \eqref{eq:phit} does not depend on $c$ and factors out.
\end{proof}

The $4/3$ cap is thus the \emph{uninformative point} of the master law. Averaging over the uniform label within each fibre kills any reading that is a function of $c$, but leaves the diagonal of a label-side likelihood intact.

\section{The which-factor ceiling}

\begin{definition}[Which-factor model]
On $(b_p,b_q,w,h)\in[K]^2\times\{\mathrm{T},\mathrm{F}\}\times[K]$ put
$\mathbb P=\frac{1}{2K^2}L(w?b_p:b_q,\,h)$ with $L\ge0$ row-stochastic. A hit is $h=b_p$. The accuracy is $\alpha=\frac1K\sum_bL(b,b)\le1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:wfphit}
$\Phit=(\alpha+1/K)/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The slice $w=\mathrm T$ contributes $\sum_{b_p,b_q}L(b_p,b_p)/(2K^2)=\alpha/2$. The slice $w=\mathrm F$ contributes $\sum_{b_q}\sum_{b_p}L(b_q,b_p)/(2K^2)=1/(2K)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Which-factor ceiling]\label{thm:wf}
At $\theta=1/K$, for every $K\ge1$ and every $L$,
\[
\Sp=S_{1/K}\Big(\frac{\alpha+1/K}{2}\Big)\le\frac{2K^2}{K^2+1}<2,
\]
with equality for the perfect hint. Moreover $2K^2/(K^2+1)\to2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Use monotonicity in $\alpha\le1$. At $\alpha=1$ the denominator is $\tfrac12(1+K^{-2})$. Finally $2K^2/(K^2+1)=2-2/(K^2+1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Canonical partition law]
For $K=2$: $\Sp(\alpha)=8/(7-2\alpha)$. In particular $\Sp(1/2)=4/3$ and $\Sp(1)=8/5<2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Isolation removes the ceiling]
A perfect hint known to speak about $p$ gives $S_{1/K}(1)=K$. The ratio to the anonymous ceiling is $(K^2+1)/(2K)$.
\end{proposition}

\section{Moduli with \texorpdfstring{$r$}{r} factors}

Let $r=s+1\ge2$. The hint speaks about one uniformly chosen factor without naming it. The law reduces to the sufficient statistic $(b_p,b_{\mathrm{other}},j,h)$ with $j$ uniform on $\{0,\dots,s\}$.

\begin{theorem}
$\Phit=(\alpha+s/K)/(s+1)$, and every likelihood satisfies
\[
\Sp\le\Phi_s(K):=\frac{(s+1)K^2}{sK^2-(s-1)K+s},
\]
with equality for the perfect hint. The denominator equals $s(K(K-1)+1)+K>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Fine-dial limit and overshoot]
For $s\ge1$, $\Phi_s(K)\to(s+1)/s=r/(r-1)$ as $K\to\infty$. For $r=2$ the limit is approached from below. For $s\ge2$, $\Phi_s(s+1)>(s+1)/s$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The overshoot inequality reduces to $(s-1)(s+1)>s$, that is, $s^2-s-1>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Binary dial optimal for $r\ge7$; collapse onto $4/3$]
$\Phi_s(2)=4(s+1)/(3s+2)=4r/(3r-1)$. If $s\ge6$, then $\Phi_s(K)\le\Phi_s(2)$ for all $K\ge1$. Moreover $4r/(3r-1)\to4/3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The inequality is equivalent to $q(K)=(s-2)K^2-4(s-1)K+4s\ge0$. The quadratic $q$ has the root $2$, its other root is $2s/(s-2)\le3$ for $s\ge6$, and $q(1)=s+2>0$.
\end{proof}

\section{The certain-hint ladder}

\begin{definition}
For $t\ge2$, $\Lambda(t)=2^{t-2}/(1-2^{1-t})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
With $a=2^{1-t}$ and $\theta_t=2a(1-a)$: $\Lambda(t)=S_{\theta_t}(1)$. Moreover $2^{t-2}<\Lambda(t)\le2^{t-1}$; $2^t/\Lambda(t)=4(1-2^{1-t})\to4$; and $\Lambda(t+1)/\Lambda(t)\to2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$1/(2a(1-a))=2^t/(4(1-a))$ and $1-a\in[1/2,1)$. For the ratio, write it as $2\cdot\frac{2^t/\Lambda(t)}{2^{t+1}/\Lambda(t+1)}$.
\end{proof}

The ladder loses exactly two bits against $2^t$ (parity and which-factor) and doubles per extra bit.

\section{Trace hints}

\begin{theorem}
Let $T(t)=2^{t-1}/C_t$ with $C_t>0$. Then $\log_2T(t)=t-1-\log_2C_t$. If $0<c_0\le C_t\le c_1$, then $\log_2T(t)/t\to1$. If $C_t\to c>0$, then $\log_2T(t+1)-\log_2T(t)\to1$.
\end{theorem}

A constant recovery overhead (for example about $5\times$) is a divisor, not a rate penalty.

\section{The noise break-even surface}

In the fallback model, $W(\theta,P,\varepsilon)=(1-\varepsilon)(1-(1-\theta)P)+\varepsilon(1+\theta)$.

\begin{theorem}[Break-even surface]
For $\theta<1$ and $\varepsilon<1$, a which-factor hint with $P=(\alpha+\theta)/2$ has $W<1$ iff
\[
\alpha>\alpha^*(\theta,\varepsilon)=\frac{2\varepsilon\theta}{(1-\varepsilon)(1-\theta)}-\theta.
\]
For $0<\theta<1$, $\alpha^*$ is nondecreasing in $\varepsilon\in[0,1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$W<1$ iff $\varepsilon\theta<(1-\varepsilon)(1-\theta)P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[External hints tolerate more noise]
For $0<\theta<1$, internal filters ($P=\theta$) are net-positive iff $\varepsilon<(1-\theta)/(2-\theta)$. Perfect unnamed external hints ($P=(1+\theta)/2$) are net-positive iff $\varepsilon<(1-\theta^2)/(1+2\theta-\theta^2)$. The second threshold is strictly larger. At $\theta=1/2$ the thresholds are $1/3$ and $3/7$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
After cancelling $1-\theta$, the comparison reduces to $1+2\theta-\theta^2<2+\theta-\theta^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
A different cost accounting in the original experimental study gave thresholds $1/6$ and $3/5$. The ordering of internal below external is the same.
\end{remark}

\section{A strategy-free guessing bound}

The target is uniform on $\mathcal A$ with $|\mathcal A|=M\ge1$. A hint is any $H:\mathcal A\to B$, and $g(x)\ge1$ is the step at which $x$ is tested. The strategy is \emph{fibre-injective}: $H(x)=H(y)$ and $g(x)=g(y)$ imply $x=y$. Set $\E[g]=\frac1M\sum g$ and $\Sp=\frac{(M+1)/2}{\E[g]}$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:distinct}
$n$ distinct positive integers have sum at least $n(n+1)/2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induct on the maximum $a$. The remaining $n-1$ elements lie in $[1,a-1]$, so $n\le a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Guessing bound]\label{thm:guess}
$M^2+|B|M\le|B|\cdot2\sum_xg(x)$, i.e.\ $\E[g]\ge\frac12(M/|B|+1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
On each fibre of size $n_b$, $g$ is injective, so Lemma~\ref{lem:distinct} gives $n_b(n_b+1)\le2\sum_{F_b}g$. Summing gives $\sum n_b^2+M\le2\sum g$. Cauchy--Schwarz gives $M^2\le|B|\sum n_b^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Linear pricing; no synergy]
$\Sp\le|B|$. Hence a hint with at most $2^t$ values gives $\Sp\le2^t$, and hints of $t_1$ and $t_2$ bits used jointly give at most $2^{t_1}2^{t_2}$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$2|B|\sum g\ge M^2+|B|M\ge M(M+1)$. For the joint statement, apply this to the product hint.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
On $[B]\times[m]$ with $H(b,i)=b$ and $g(b,i)=i+1$, equality holds: $B\cdot2\sum g=(Bm)^2+B(Bm)$. The speedup is then $B(M+1)/(M+B)\to B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Isolation cost]
Separating $M$ candidates with $q$ yes/no queries requires $M\le2^q$, i.e.\ $q\ge\lceil\log_2M\rceil$, and $\lceil\log_2M\rceil$ queries suffice.
\end{theorem}

With $M=\pi(\sqrt N)$ this is the $\log_2\pi(\sqrt N)$ price of breaking the which-factor ceiling ($17$ queries for a $40$-bit $N$). The original experiments found it net-positive from $t=5$.

\section{Algorithms}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(A)] \emph{Evaluate a filter:} compute $\Phit$ in $O(|\Omega|)$ and return $S_\theta(\Phit)$.
\item[(B)] \emph{Optimal hinted guessing:} rank candidates within each fibre. This is optimal fibre by fibre, and tight for balanced hints.
\item[(C)] \emph{Best dial:} maximise $\Phi_{r-1}(K)$. For $r\ge7$ return $K=2$.
\item[(D)] \emph{Noise test:} compare $\alpha$ with $\alpha^*(\theta,\varepsilon)$ in $O(1)$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Empirical corroboration}
The original study reported: deviation at most $0.0032$ from the partition law across $\alpha$ ($m=31$, $400{,}000$ trials); a pointwise-exact $\chi(c)$-split for internal readings; maximum deviation $0.0089$ in exhaustive runs $m=3,\dots,8$; ladder ratios in $[0.9986,1.0045]$; and $20/20$ agreement on break-even verdicts. An early experiment that scored hits in the wrong label space produced a speedup flat in $\alpha$. This is exactly the signature Theorem~\ref{thm:internal} predicts for readings that depend only on the observable coordinate.

\section{Discussion: the completed barrier map}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Source & Price & Status\\
\midrule
Residues (internal) & $\le 4/3$ (uninformative point) & theorem\\
Position (structural) & $\approx5.19\times$ & measured\\
External hints & $\le2^t$; $<2\times$ per dial unless named & theorem\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

One scalar, the hit probability, prices every filter. Fibre uniformity explains why internal information is capped and external information is not. Work bits add up without synergy.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Partial naming:} if the hint names its factor with probability $\beta$, we conjecture the best speedup is $S_{1/K}(\beta+(1-\beta)(1+(r-1)/K)/r)$.
\item \emph{Non-uniform priors:} extend Theorem~\ref{thm:guess} along the lines of Ar\i kan's guessing inequality, using R\'enyi entropy of order $1/2$.
\item Reconcile the noise thresholds $1/6,3/5$ with an explicit cost model.
\item Prove the $t=5$ isolation break-even from explicit prime-counting bounds.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
A filtered search is priced by its hit probability. Internal readings are pinned at chance, which makes $4/3$ the speedup of a coin flip. Anonymous external hints stay below $2\times$ per dial, and every $t$-bit hint, under every strategy, buys at most $2^t$. External information is priced linearly in bits.

\end{document}

